Give the kernel a minimal AMFI, so that dyld and launchd can start.

With launchd and its 37 dylibs on the RAM disk, the boot now gets as far as
running dyld, and dyld stops on its own first question:

    panic(cpu 0 caller 0x...): initproc exited -- exit reason namespace 6
    subcode 0x9 description: amfi_check_dyld_policy_self() failed

Namespace 6 is OS_REASON_DYLD, so that description is dyld's own halt message
rather than anything the kernel decided.

amfi_check_dyld_policy_self() is __mac_syscall("AMFI", 0x5a, &args), and
security/mac_base.c answers that with

    error = ENOPOLICY;
    for (...) if (strcmp(mpc->mpc_name, target) == 0 && ...)

On a real device "AMFI" is AppleMobileFileIntegrity, a prelinked kext. This
kernel has no kexts, so no policy carries that name and the call can never
return anything but ENOPOLICY. dyld treats that as fatal and halts before it
has opened a single library.

So a policy by that name is registered. It is modelled on vnguard, which is
already in this tree and already loads on this board -- "Security policy
loaded: Guarded vnode policy (vnguard)" is on the console -- so the shape is
not invented: a mac_policy_conf, a mac_policy_register() call, and an init
function called from kernel_bootstrap() beside vnguard's, long before
bsd_init() goes looking for an init program.

The call number and the argument block are read out of dyld rather than
guessed at:

    _amfi_check_dyld_policy_self:
        cbz  x1, ...                ; no output pointer -> EINVAL
        str  xzr, [x1]              ; *output_flags = 0
        add  x8, sp, #8             ; an uninitialised local
        stp  x0, x8, [sp, #0x10]    ; { input_flags, &local }
        add  x0, x0, #0x647         ; "AMFI"
        mov  w1, #0x5a
        add  x2, sp, #0x10
        bl   ___mac_syscall
        cbz  w0, ...                ; on success, *output_flags = local

Two 64-bit words: the input flags, and a user address to write the answer to.
And the local at sp+8 is never initialised before the call -- so a policy that
returned success without writing to it would hand dyld whatever was on its
stack and let it be read as a set of permissions. That is the one thing this
had to get right, and it is why the function was disassembled instead of
approximated.

The answer is zero, which is what AMFI gives an ordinary platform binary.
Every bit in that word grants a relaxation -- honouring @executable_path and
@loader_path, DYLD_ environment variables, fallback search paths, a custom
shared cache -- and refusing all of them is both the restrictive choice and
the ordinary one on a device. dyld says as much itself, in a string a few
bytes from the one above:

    dyld: warning: @executable_path/ ignored because of amfi policy

amfi_dyld_flags= overrides it, for when something turns out to need one. The
bit assignments belong to AMFI and are deliberately not written down in the
patch: this kernel has no way to check a guess about them, and a comment
asserting the wrong ones would be worse than none.

Once dyld ran and launchd started a daemon, the same absence surfaced a
second time:

    initproc exited -- exit reason namespace 7 subcode 0x1 description:
    unexpected exec of non-platform binary: /usr/sbin/syslogd

launchd had spawned /usr/libexec/xpcproxy and xpcproxy had exec'd syslogd;
what launchd refused was the result. A daemon named by a system LaunchDaemons
plist has to be a platform binary, and nothing in this kernel could make one.
That status comes from a single bit in ubc_cs_blob_add():

    if (blob->csb_flags & CS_PLATFORM_BINARY) {
            blob->csb_platform_binary = 1;

and the only thing that can set it is the policy hook immediately above,
which is handed the flags by reference so that a policy can add to them. That
hook is AMFI's on a device. So this policy answers it too, and answers yes.

Yes for everything sounds worse than it is. On a device the answer comes from
the signature, and Apple's code is platform code because its signature says
so. This kernel cannot tell one signature from another: the SHA that would
distinguish them returns zeros, because there is no corecrypto. The
distinction does not exist here, so refusing to draw it gives nothing away.
amfi_platform_binary=0 turns it off.

What this is not: it is not AMFI. It answers one question and implements no
part of what the name stands for. Nothing here validates a code signature, an
entitlement or a provisioning profile, and nothing here is a step toward
doing so -- that work belongs with corecrypto, which this kernel also does
not have. It exists so that dyld can get past a call that cannot otherwise be
answered, on a kernel that has no code signing to enforce in the first place.
A real iOS userland will need the real thing; this gets the loader running.


The first attempt at that was not enough: the same panic came back with the
hook in place. So launchd was disassembled too, to find out what it actually
asks rather than assume it:

    mov  w1, #0                      ; ops = CS_OPS_STATUS
    bl   _csops_audittoken
    cbz  w0, 0x1000205ec             ; on success ->
    bl   ___error
    ldr  w8, [x0]
    cmp  w8, #0x3                    ; ESRCH: the process is gone, let it be
    b.eq <return>
    ...
    adr  x2, "csops returned %d"     ; any other errno: logged, then fatal
    ...
  0x1000205ec:
    ldur w8, [fp, #-0x24]            ; the status word csops wrote
    tbnz w8, #0x1a, <return>         ; bit 26 set -> accepted
    ...                              ; clear -> "unexpected exec of
                                     ;   non-platform binary: %s: %s [%d]"

Bit 26 is CS_PLATFORM_BINARY. That is worth knowing for what it rules out as
much as for what it confirms: launchd does not compare a cdhash, so this is
not blocked on corecrypto, and the chain it does depend on is the one this
policy already writes to --

    amfi_stub_vnode_check_signature()  sets CS_PLATFORM_BINARY in cs_flags
    ubc_cs_blob_add()                  turns that into csb_platform_binary
    csproc_get_platform_binary()       reads it back off the text vnode
    csops(CS_OPS_STATUS)               ORs it into the word launchd reads

-- with every link of it read and found correct. Something in it does not
join up, and reading further has stopped paying: both ends are known and
nothing between them is conditional in a way that explains it.

So this adds a trace instead of another guess. amfi_debug=1 prints what the
signature hook is asked and what it answers, and -- through
mpo_proc_check_get_cs_info, which csops_internal() calls on the way to
CS_OPS_STATUS -- prints the kernel's answer at the moment launchd asks for
it:

    amfi: signature of syslogd: cs_flags 0x0 -> 0x4000000
    amfi: pid 1 asked csops(0) about pid 23[syslogd]: platform=1

Two lines that put the break on one side or the other. It is a boot-arg of
its own rather than a use of cs_debug because cs_debug > 1 also turns on
cs_validate_page()'s per-page report, and on this kernel every page fails to
validate -- that is corecrypto's absence, and it fills the console faster
than it can be read.

mac_policy_register()'s return value is now checked as well. It was ignored,
which would have made a registration failure silent: the boot would carry on
and die in dyld, with nothing on the console connecting the two.
